# site_v2 per-entity export build artifact (#365)
artifacts/site_data/

# site_v2 real-data build: the site build enumerates src/data/{teams,fixtures}/*.json. Only the
# committed samples are tracked; a full export populates these dirs at build time (100s MB to >1 GB)
# and must never be committed. Keep the samples, ignore the bulk.
#
# THE SAMPLE SET MUST BE SELF-CONSISTENT (#367). The landing page links to the next matchday,
# and `audit-seo.mjs` fails the build on any internal link that does not resolve to an emitted page.
# So every entity the committed `landing.json` points at needs its payload here, or CI goes red
# while a local build with a full export stays green -- which is exactly the trap this list sprang
# the first time. ~1.7 MB total: samples, not bulk.
#
# Six team payloads (132, 463, 587, 1607, 2242, 2759) were tracked here until the trending block was
# cut on 2026-08-08. They were listed ONLY because trending rows linked to them; with that block
# gone nothing on the site links to a team page, so they emitted six orphans. 33.json stays -- it is
# the team-page's own sample and predates the landing work.
#
# ⚠ REGENERATE AS A SET. `landing.json` is a snapshot of whatever fixtures are next, so refreshing
# it changes WHICH ids belong below. Refresh the landing sample and these entries together, or the
# next build fails on links to ids that are no longer here.
# Export byproducts: `export_site_data.py` writes both into its output dir on every run. Neither is
# part of the tracked sample, and both turned up as untracked strays the first time this sample was
# refreshed from a full export. Ignored rather than deleted, so the next refresh does not re-arm the
# same stray-file gate.
site_v2/src/data/manifest.json
site_v2/src/data/slug_map.json
site_v2/src/data/teams/*.json
!site_v2/src/data/teams/33.json
site_v2/src/data/fixtures/*.json
# The 2026-09-01 matchday: 4 fixtures across 3 competitions, exactly what `landing.json` links to.
#
# ⛔ THIS SET REPLACED THE 2026-08-28 ONE WHOLESALE, and a future refresh must do the same rather
# than append. The export emits UPCOMING fixtures only — `status_short in ('NS', 'TBD') and
# fixture_date >= current_date()` in `fetch_fixture_payloads` — and once a fixture kicks off its
# pre-match form rows leave `mart_team_momentum` and `mart_team_season_record` altogether. So a past
# id can never be re-exported, and a stale entry left here would pin a payload that no rerun can
# ever update. Replace, never add.
# ⚠ The 2026-08-28 set proved that from the other side: it was FOUR DAYS past when replaced, and its
# payloads were still missing four mart columns added after they were written — `shots_inside_box_pct`,
# `finishing_efficiency_pct`, `passes_accuracy_pct`, `passes_key_per_match` — so the fixture
# comparison rendered 12 of its 16 locked rows. Not one of the 19 changed on a rerun. Zero of them
# carried over into this set.
!site_v2/src/data/fixtures/1550704.json
!site_v2/src/data/fixtures/1603007.json
!site_v2/src/data/fixtures/1628894.json
!site_v2/src/data/fixtures/1629056.json

# CPO working documents dropped in the repo ROOT (exports of planning conversations, briefs).
# Not project source, so not committed -- and not deleted either: the contract and stop gates flag
# any untracked stray, and the stop gate's suggested remedy ("delete untracked strays") would
# destroy his file. Content worth keeping is distilled into a GitHub issue instead; the Gemini
# company-building conversation became #868.
# ROOT-ANCHORED on purpose. A bare `*.pdf` would silently hide a PDF anywhere in the tree, including
# one a future task legitimately wants tracked.
/*.pdf
